= Knowledge market — Simple =

A '''knowledge market''' is a system for matching people or organizations that need knowledge with people, organizations, or resources that can supply it.

The exchange can involve money, but it does not have to. People may share expertise in return for reputation, reciprocal help, professional recognition, access to other resources, or support for a shared mission.

Examples include consulting, expert networks, patent licensing, research services, internal question-answering systems, and technology-transfer programs.

== Buyers, sellers and brokers ==

A knowledge market can be described using three roles:

* a '''buyer''' has a question, problem, or need;
* a '''seller''' has relevant knowledge or can produce it;
* a '''broker''' helps the two sides find and evaluate one another.

A broker might be a person, a professional network, a platform, a technology-transfer office, or a search system.

The difficult part is often not creating knowledge but discovering who has it and whether it can be trusted.

== Knowledge is different from an ordinary product ==

Knowledge goods have unusual economic properties.

Once a fact or method is known, one person's use usually does not prevent another person from using it. Copying a digital report can also be extremely cheap even if producing the research was expensive.

At the same time, a buyer may not know whether an answer is valuable until after seeing enough of it to evaluate it. If the seller reveals everything first, there may be nothing left to sell.

These problems make knowledge markets more complicated than ordinary markets for physical goods.<ref name="arrow">[https://www.nber.org/books-and-chapters/rate-and-direction-inventive-activity-economic-and-social-factors/economic-welfare-and-allocation-resources-invention Arrow, K. J. (1962), “Economic Welfare and the Allocation of Resources for Invention.”]</ref>

== Money is not the only incentive ==

Inside organizations, people often exchange knowledge without direct payment.

Motives can include:

* reciprocity;
* reputation;
* helping colleagues;
* professional identity;
* promotion;
* shared goals.

Rewards can help participation, but badly designed rewards can encourage people to post low-quality answers or can reduce voluntary cooperation.<ref name="serrat">[https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-10-0983-9_110 Serrat, O. (2017), “On Internal Knowledge Markets.”]</ref>

== Quality is a separate problem ==

A functioning market can match a buyer with a seller without proving that the seller is correct.

A highly rated expert can still be wrong. A popular answer can be unsupported. A patent can have market value without proving that a scientific claim is true.

Knowledge markets therefore need separate quality controls such as:

* evidence;
* independent review;
* reputation based on real outcomes;
* provenance;
* correction mechanisms;
* conflict-of-interest disclosure.

'''Price and popularity are not measures of truth.'''

== Markets, commons and open knowledge ==

Not all knowledge should be exchanged through prices.

Open-source software, public libraries, open science, and collaborative encyclopedias use shared infrastructure, norms, public funding, reputation, and volunteer contribution.

These systems may solve some of the same coordination problems without operating as ordinary markets.

== Knowledge market versus prediction market ==

A knowledge market exchanges expertise or knowledge resources.

A prediction market trades contracts about uncertain future events and uses prices to aggregate beliefs.

Prediction markets can produce useful information, but they are not the same thing as knowledge markets.

== Artificial intelligence ==

AI can make knowledge markets cheaper by helping people search, summarize, translate, or draft answers.

It can also create large amounts of low-cost incorrect content.

A knowledge market using AI should preserve provenance: users should be able to tell whether an answer came from a human expert, an AI model, retrieved evidence, or a combination.

